import RindIsa.AArch64

/-!
# The main loops of aws-lc's eight-way AES-192-GCM kernels

The bodies of the main loops of rind's naked functions
`low::aarch64::aes_gcm::aesv8_gcm_8x_enc_192` and `aesv8_gcm_8x_dec_192`,
aws-lc's `aesv8_gcm_8x_enc_192` and `aesv8_gcm_8x_dec_192`
(`crypto/fipsmodule/modes/asm/aesv8-gcm-armv8-unroll8.pl`), in rind's model
of AArch64: the instructions from each loop's label to the `b.lt` back to
it, in aws-lc's order, the bytes of `low/aarch64/aws_lc/aesv8_gcm_8x_{enc,dec}_192.linux.s`
between those lines. Each iteration runs AES-192's twelve rounds on eight
counter blocks (`v0`–`v7`, the round keys loaded from `x11` into `v26`–`v28`
by turns) while it hashes the eight blocks of ciphertext the iteration
before left in `v8`–`v15` into the running GHASH in `v19`, by the powers of
H that `x6`'s table holds, with one reduction by the constant at `x10`; it
then loads the next eight input blocks from `x0`, adds the keystream, and
stores the eight output blocks at `x2`.

The functions themselves are not yet programs here: aws-lc's tails enter a
shared chain of single-block steps from eight places, which `Code`'s `ite`
and `loop` cannot lay out, so `admin/prove lean` does not yet compare these
bodies with rind's build. `Proven.lean` lists them for `admin/prove vg` to
cosimulate their instructions, and the lines they transcribe are named
below.
-/

namespace Rind.AArch64.AesGcm8x192

open VG VG.AArch64

def vop (o : VOp) : Instr := .vg (.vop o)

